  "question": {
    "text": "The metal that cannot be obtained by the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of its salt is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "For the reduction of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> at cathode,<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1.23</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math><br /><br />For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ca</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>red</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2.87</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math><br /><br />i.e., water will be reduced&nbsp;at the cathode, in preference to the liberation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi></math>.<br /><br />However, in the case of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cu</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cr</mi></math>,&nbsp;their standard reduction potentials are higher than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and will be liberated/ deposited at the cathode and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> will not be reduced."
  }
